{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>He is your praise.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): it is fitting that He be your praise, that you not praise any power but Him alone, may He be exalted. Or Scripture may say: 'He is your praise' and 'He is your Elohim' — He is your praise, that He is your Elohim." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'He is your praise, the Elohim of Israel' — and He is 'your Elohim,' the designated Name. And thus Scripture reveals to you that Israel are given over to these two attributes, as it says (Deuteronomy 26:19): 'and to make you supreme for praise (תהלה) and for name (שם) and for glory (תפארת)' — for praise, which is the Name; and for glory, the designated Name. And the intention is to say that the praise, which is the Name — in it is the glory: the attribute of mercy within the attribute of judgment, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 89): 'for the glory of their strength is You.'" "<b>And reflect</b> on the matter the prophet said (Isaiah 48:9): 'for My name's sake I will defer My anger, and for My praise (תהלתי) I will restrain (אחטם) it for you, so as not to cut you off.' I shall show you the light of the verse: 'the חוטם' is a sign of acceptance, and it is as though He said: 'I will accept the fragrance of the offerings,' in the manner of what is said (Deuteronomy 33:10): 'they shall place incense in Your nostril (באפך).' For the incense belongs to the attribute of judgment called תהלה. And it employed this expression in the opposite of acceptance: ריח — 'and he presses his nose (חוטמו) in his hand,' and it is a sign of rejection, in the manner of (Leviticus 26:31): 'and I will not smell (אריח) the fragrance of your sweet savors.' And after it is written (Isaiah 48:10–11): 'for My sake, for My sake I will do, for how could it be profaned' — that is, if I were to cut you off, My name would be profaned; and it is in the manner of what Joshua said (Joshua 7:9): 'and they will cut off our name from the earth, and what will You do for Your great name.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {2 "In comment 2, the phrase 'והזכיר הלשון הזה בהפך מן הקץ בריח שהוא דוחק חוטמו בידו' is textually difficult; read hyper-literally. 'בהפך מן הקץ בריח' may mean 'in the opposite of acceptance: ריח (reish-yod-chet),' and 'דוחק חוטמו בידו' ('he presses his nose in his hand') appears to be an idiomatic gesture of rejection, though the idiom is unusual and may reflect textual corruption."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:47:25.383659259Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}